Applied to the javacc 7.0.13 output by //build-tools/bazel:javacc.bzl.

javacc reports end of input by throwing java.io.IOException out of FillBuff(), and
AstParserTokenManager swallows it at every catch site to produce the EOF token. Allocating that
exception per throw is the single largest cost of parsing a short query, because fillInStackTrace()
walks AstParser's whole recursive-descent stack. Only the EOF sentinel is shared here; an
IOException from a genuine read failure is still the one the reader threw.

Regenerate this file whenever the javacc version pinned in MODULE.bazel changes: build
//jackson-jq-core:ast_parser_srcs, copy the generated SimpleCharStream.java into a/ and an edited
b/, and run `diff -u --label a/SimpleCharStream.java --label b/SimpleCharStream.java`.

--- a/SimpleCharStream.java
+++ b/SimpleCharStream.java
@@ -87,6 +87,16 @@
     tokenBegin = 0;
   }
 
+  /**
+   * javacc signals end of input by throwing from every readChar() past the end of the input, and
+   * the recursive-descent parser's stack is deep when that happens, so filling in a stack trace
+   * per throw costs more than the rest of tokenizing a short query. The signal carries no state
+   * and never escapes AstParserTokenManager, which swallows it at every catch site, so one shared
+   * instance does the same job. The stack trace it holds is the one captured at class
+   * initialization and is meaningless; nothing reads it.
+   */
+  private static final java.io.IOException END_OF_INPUT = new java.io.IOException();
+
   protected void FillBuff() throws java.io.IOException
   {
     if (maxNextCharInd == available)
@@ -116,7 +126,7 @@
       if ((i = inputStream.read(buffer, maxNextCharInd, available - maxNextCharInd)) == -1)
       {
         inputStream.close();
-        throw new java.io.IOException();
+        throw END_OF_INPUT;
       }
       else
         maxNextCharInd += i;
